{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Config-driven status mapping (map-or-drift, collapse round-trip via rebar-status labels)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nStatus mapping is hardcoded across the Jira-family outbound/inbound paths. Outbound,\nthe local→Jira status name is produced from the built-in default\n`adapters/jira_family/value_maps.LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` at **four** consumer sites (see\nScope), and mirrored a fifth time as `config.local_to_jira_status`. Inbound recovery\ninverts `config.jira_to_local_status` / `inbound_fields._JIRA_TO_LOCAL_STATUS` and\nprefers the `rebar-status:<local>` annotation label via\n`inbound_fields._REBAR_STATUS_LABEL_TO_LOCAL`. The `rebar-status:` label mechanism is\ntoday hardcoded to the two non-injective statuses `blocked`/`cancelled`\n(`outbound_labels._STATUS_ANNOTATION_LABEL` stamps them; the inbound maps recover them).\n\nA Jira project whose workflow lacks a rebar status (e.g. no `Blocked`) has no echo-safe\nway to remap it, and a config-driven collapse over statuses **other** than\nblocked/cancelled has no label to stamp or recover. Status must become config-driven on\nthe S1 mapping core: always **map-or-drift** (never skip a whole ticket on status), and\nany non-injective collapse (two rebar statuses → one Jira status) must round-trip via the\n`rebar-status:` annotation so inbound recovers the original rebar status.\n\n## Dependency (prerequisite ticket)\n\nThis story wires the status axis onto the mapping core delivered by S1,\n**[rebar:bfe7-0900-c3d1-4141]** (merged to `main`). S1's `rebar_reconciler.mapping_config`\nprovides exactly the surface used here:\n\n- `load_mapping_config(root=None) -> MappingConfig` — reads the reserved `[mapping]`\n  section (user←project shallow merge).\n- `resolve_for_project(config, project_key, *, builtin) -> MappingLayer` — merges\n  `builtin ← [mapping.default] ← [mapping.projects.<KEY>]`; axis maps deep-merge per key,\n  vocab replaces wholesale most-specific-wins.\n- `MappingLayer.status_map: Mapping[str, str]` — the resolved local→Jira status map; a\n  value of `mapping_config.SKIP` means \"intentionally drop\"; a local status **absent**\n  from the map is the drift case.\n- `validate(resolved, capability)` — offline fail-closed check (unused here beyond\n  construction; status is always capability-present).\n\nThe built-in `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` is passed as the `builtin` argument to\n`resolve_for_project`, so with **no** `[mapping]` config the effective map is byte-for-byte\ntoday's default and every existing behaviour and parity test is unchanged.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd ONE resolution seam and thread the resolved map through the existing consumers rather\nthan duplicating resolution at each.\n\n1. **`config.py` — status resolution entry point.** Add\n   `effective_status_map(project_key: str | None, root=None) -> dict[str, str]` and its\n   inverse `effective_jira_to_local(project_key, root=None) -> dict[str, str]`, each\n   calling `mapping_config.load_mapping_config` + `resolve_for_project` with\n   `builtin=LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` (forward) / the canonical `jira_to_local_status`\n   (reverse is NOT a naive inversion — it is the canonical preimage, so the reverse\n   entry point layers only the config's explicit reverse overrides onto the canonical\n   map). `local_to_jira_status` / `jira_to_local_status` remain as the built-in literals\n   these functions fall back to (kept in lock-step with `value_maps` by the existing\n   parity test). `config.py` imports `mapping_config` **lazily inside the function**\n   (preserving its stdlib-only top-level rule).\n\n2. **Outbound consumers read the resolved map.** The project key is resolved once per\n   ticket in `outbound_differ` (where `projects_store.resolve_project(ticket, mapping)`\n   already runs) and threaded to the value mappers as an optional\n   `status_map: dict[str,str] | None` parameter; when `None` the mappers fall back to the\n   built-in `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` (no behaviour change on the untouched call paths). The\n   consumer sites:\n   - `adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.py` `OutboundFieldMapper.map_fields_to_remote`\n     (shared UPDATE path, Cloud+DC delegate) — line ~91.\n   - `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py` `_map_local_to_jira_fields` (Cloud CREATE) — ~134.\n   - `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` create-field mapping — ~80.\n   - `adapters/jira/acli.py` transition-name resolution — ~149.\n\n3. **Drift = leave Jira as-is, never a per-mutation failure.** When the effective map has\n   **no entry** for a local status (and no built-in fallback value), the outbound mapper\n   **omits the `status` field** from the produced mutation (rebar keeps its own status;\n   Jira is left unchanged) and records a **drift alert** (see Testing for the recorder).\n   This replaces today's `.get(status, \"To Do\")` silent-coerce-to-`To Do` and the\n   preflight/applier per-mutation FAILURE path: `reconcile_helpers.preflight_status_mapping`\n   is updated so a status that is unmapped **but marked drift-eligible** is not counted as\n   an offending mutation (the empty-map kill-switch and inbound-skip are preserved).\n\n4. **Generalised `rebar-status:` collapse round-trip.** Replace the hardcoded\n   blocked/cancelled label maps with derivation from the effective map:\n   - Outbound (`outbound_labels._STATUS_ANNOTATION_LABEL` / `_diff_status_annotation_labels`):\n     compute the **collapse set** = local statuses that share a Jira target with another\n     local status under the effective map; for any local status in the collapse set stamp\n     `rebar-status:<local>` (blocked/cancelled remain a special case of this rule under the\n     default map, so the default behaviour is preserved).\n   - Inbound (`inbound_fields._REBAR_STATUS_LABEL_TO_LOCAL`, and its parity copies in\n     `inbound_translate.py` / `apply_inbound_events.py`): recover the local status by\n     **parsing the `rebar-status:<local>` suffix** and validating `<local>` against the\n     effective reverse vocabulary, rather than a fixed two-entry dict. Label-first\n     precedence is unchanged; the fixed-dict copies become a single shared parse helper so\n     the four consumer sites cannot drift.\n\n5. **Parity preserved.** `value_maps.LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` and `config.local_to_jira_status`\n   stay the built-in literal (the injected `builtin`), keeping the existing\n   `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_config.py` parity tests green.\n\n## Scope (files)\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/config.py` — add `effective_status_map` /\n  `effective_jira_to_local` resolution entry points (lazy `mapping_config` import).\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_differ.py` — resolve project key once and\n  thread the effective `status_map` into the value mappers + label diff.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.py` — accept\n  optional `status_map`, drift on unmapped (omit field), fall back to built-in when `None`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py` — same for the\n  Cloud CREATE path.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` — same for the\n  DC CREATE path.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli.py` — resolve transition name via\n  the effective map.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_labels.py` — derive the collapse set from\n  the effective map; generalise `rebar-status:` stamping beyond blocked/cancelled.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py` — label-first recovery by parsing\n  the `rebar-status:<local>` suffix against the effective reverse vocabulary.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/inbound_translate.py` — share the parse helper\n  (retire the duplicated `_REBAR_STATUS_LABEL_TO_LOCAL` literal).\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_events.py` — use the shared parse\n  helper (retire its duplicated literal).\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_status_mapping.py` (new) — behavioral oracle.\n\n## Testing\n\nTest tier: **in-process unit** — outbound/inbound status translation are pure functions\nover the resolved map; the oracle is the produced target field / omitted field / stamped\nlabel / recovered status / recorded drift alert. Tests exercise the REAL entry points\n(`config.effective_status_map`, the outbound mappers, `outbound_labels._diff_status_annotation_labels`,\n`inbound_fields._map_jira_to_local_fields`), never re-implemented copies.\n\n- **Config-effect contrast (real path):** with a `default.status_map` remapping\n  `blocked→\"To Do\"`, assert the outbound mapper targets `To Do` for a blocked ticket; with\n  no `[mapping]` override, assert it targets the built-in default — same read path, config\n  the only difference. `test_status_config_effect`.\n- **Drift (failure path):** an unmapped local status causes the outbound mapper to **omit**\n  the `status` field (Jira left unchanged) and record the drift alert; asserted on the\n  produced mutation (no `status` key) and the recorded alert, not a crash and not a\n  coerced `To Do`. `test_unmapped_status_drifts`.\n- **Collapse round-trip (generalised):** a config map collapsing two rebar statuses onto\n  one Jira status stamps each `rebar-status:<local>` outbound; inbound recovers each\n  distinct rebar status by parsing the label — asserted both directions, over a NON\n  blocked/cancelled status pair so it proves generalisation. `test_status_collapse_roundtrip`.\n- **Parity:** the existing `value_maps`↔`config` parity tests\n  (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_config.py`) stay green (run them).\n\n### Drift alert provenance\n\nThe drift alert is emitted through the **same non-fatal stderr-warning channel** that\n`reconcile_helpers.preflight_status_mapping` already uses for unmapped statuses (a\n`reconcile: … status … drifted (Jira left unchanged)` line to `stderr`), so no new\nalert type/subsystem is introduced. The unit oracle asserts on the captured stderr (via\n`capsys`) plus the omitted `status` field; no live reconcile pass is required.\n\n## Rollback / expand-contract\n\nThe behaviour change (unmapped status: coerce-to-`To Do`/fail → drift-and-leave-as-is) is\ngated by config only: with no `[mapping]` section the effective map equals the built-in\ndefault, which maps every rebar status, so **no status is ever unmapped** and the drift\nbranch is unreachable — today's behaviour is bit-for-bit preserved. Back-out is reverting\nthis change; no data migration is involved because the `rebar-status:` label format is\nunchanged (only its stamping/recovery becomes config-derived, a superset of the old\nfixed set).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Effective status map = built-in `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` ← `[mapping.default].status_map`\n  ← `[mapping.projects.<KEY>].status_map` via S1 `resolve_for_project`; a non-default\n  overlay changes the outbound target through the real mapper path — verified by\n  `test_status_mapping.py::test_status_config_effect`.\n- [ ] An unmapped status **drifts**: the outbound mutation omits the `status` field (Jira\n  left unchanged) and a non-fatal drift warning is recorded; the ticket is never skipped and\n  never recorded as a per-mutation failure — verified by\n  `test_status_mapping.py::test_unmapped_status_drifts`.\n- [ ] A config-collapsed status pair (including statuses OTHER than blocked/cancelled)\n  round-trips losslessly via `rebar-status:<local>` labels — verified by\n  `test_status_mapping.py::test_status_collapse_roundtrip`.\n- [ ] The `value_maps`↔`config` parity tests in\n  `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_config.py` remain green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787332094753555001,"uuid":"deb24fa9-c638-44ba-ba93-aa0faaec9faa"}